body_text:
### Description

In 
https://www.keycloak.org/server/hostname#_defining_specific_parts_of_the_hostname_option
is said that `hostname` full uri may include a context path (`/auth` in the example). 

The doc, as @shawkins  points out in https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/discussions/52373#discussioncomment-18288297 
"is trying to briefly cover the case where the proxy has a context path, but keycloak is not configured for one. The additional assumption is that the proxy is stripping that context path before passing the request to keycloak, so the server in this case does not need to have the relative path set."

### Value Proposition

I propose to explicitly state (possibly with an example of reverse proxy conf) that setting a context path in full url hostname configuration requires that the reverse proxy strips that context path, or that additionally an `http-relative-path` has to be set in order that configuration to work.

### Goals

Clarify the documentation about setting a context path into hostname, its usage and implications in revproxy settings, avoiding wasting time with trials and errors.

### Non-Goals

Make the documentation too cumbersome

### Discussion

https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/discussions/52373

### Notes

nginx revproxy sample fragment, in order to strip away the context path:
```
        location ~ ^/auth/ {
            #strip away the /auth context path
            rewrite ^/auth(/.*)$ $1 break;

            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Server $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
            proxy_set_header Host $http_host;

            proxy_pass https://keycloak;
        }
```

