Hi, I hit a race in the eviction path on master. I've checked the healthy-pod delete block here:

https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/3551fa84d739d7e0ceb1954025d61c43e504e97f/pkg/registry/core/pod/storage/eviction.go#L291-L305

```go
// pkg/registry/core/pod/storage/eviction.go (L291-L305, master @ 3551fa8)
deleteOptions := originalDeleteOptions

// Set deleteOptions.Preconditions.ResourceVersion to ensure
// the pod hasn't been considered healthy (ready) since we calculated
if updateDeletionOptions {
    // Take a copy so we can compare to client-provied Options later.
    deleteOptions = deleteOptions.DeepCopy()
    setPreconditionsResourceVersion(deleteOptions, &pod.ResourceVersion)
}

// Try the delete
err = addConditionAndDeletePod(r, ctx, eviction.Name, rest.ValidateAllObjectFunc, deleteOptions)
```

Root cause: `updateDeletionOptions` is only set to `true` inside the `!podutil.IsPodReady(pod)` branch (unhealthy cases). The healthy-pod path that just passed `checkAndDecrement` falls through with it still `false`, so the guard above is skipped and `deleteOptions` stays as `originalDeleteOptions` with no RV precondition when it hits `Delete`.

What that means:
1. Pod is Running and Ready, covered by PDB with `disruptionsAllowed=1`.
2. `POST /api/v1/namespaces/default/pods/foo/eviction` passes `checkAndDecrement`.
3. Before the delete, the PDB is tightened to `disruptionsAllowed=0` or the pod changes.
4. Delete still succeeds because there's no RV pin. It allows one excess disruption.

The unhealthy path already does `DeepCopy + setPreconditionsResourceVersion(pod.RV)`. I'd copy that pattern to the healthy path too and re-fetch on conflict.

Doesn't need a KEP, it's a small fix in `Create`. Happy to put up a PR if you agree with the approach.

